            <h1>Mise en Place (Prep and Organize</h1>
            <p>
             .   Measure and chop: Cut all your vegetables, meats, and herbs before turning on the heat so everything is ready to go.<br>
                • Group by cooking time: Keep dense items (carrots, potatoes) separate from fast-cooking or delicate items (spinach,
                soft herbs, seafood).<br>
                • Pat proteins dry: Dry meat, poultry, or fish with a paper towel before searing to get a rich brown crust instead of
                steaming the food in its own juice
            </p>
            <h2>Build the Flavor Base (Aromatics and Fats)</h2>
            <p>
                • Preheat the pan: Heat your pan or pot before adding oil so the food does not stick.
                • Add fat: Use a neutral oil for high-heat searing or butter/olive oil for gentle cooking (aim for 350–375°F for frying
                or 400–450°F for searing).
                • Layer aromatics: Start with onions, garlic, ginger, or spices (like cumin and turmeric). Cook dry spices briefly until
                fragrant, and add garlic near the end of this phase so it does not burn and turn bitter.
            </p>
            <h3>Cook the Main Ingredients </h3>
            <p>
                • Order matters: Add the ingredients that take the longest to cook first (like hard root vegetables or chunks of beef).
                Add quick-cooking items later.
                • Avoid overcrowding: Leave space between pieces of meat or vegetables in the pan so they brown properly rather than
                boiling in trapped steam.
                • Resist the urge to stir constantly: Let food sit undisturbed to develop a deep, caramelized flavor crust.
                
                Step 4: Simmer, Sauce, or Finish
                
                • Add liquids: Pour in broth, coconut milk, crushed tomatoes, or cream if your dish requires a braise or a sauce.
                • Simmer gently: Let the flavors meld together over low-to-medium heat.
                • Taste as you go: Dip a spoon in to check the salt, acid (like lemon juice or vinegar), and spice levels before you
                finish cooking.
                
                Step 5: Rest and Plate
                
                • Rest your meat: If you cooked steak, chicken, or a roast, pull it off the heat a few minutes early (accounting for
                carryover cooking) and let it rest for 5 to 15 minutes so the juices stay locked inside.<br>
                • Garnish and serve: Top with fresh herbs, a squeeze of citrus, or a sprinkle of cheese right before serving to brighten
                the plate.<br>
